{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12629","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12629","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12629","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihong","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Zhihong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuanyi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chuanyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hongyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhi","familyName":"jin","name":"jin, Zhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ge","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Songlin","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Songlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Ensembling Large Language Models for Code Vulnerability Detection: An Empirical Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T03:48:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:20:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-18T01:26:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-19T00:13:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Code vulnerability detection is crucial for ensuring the security and reliability of modern software systems. Recently, Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown promising capabilities in this domain. However, notable discrepancies in detection results often arise when analyzing identical code segments across different training stages of the same model or among architecturally distinct LLMs. While such inconsistencies may compromise detection stability, they also highlight a key opportunity: the latent complementarity among models can be harnessed through ensemble learning to create more robust vulnerability detection systems. In this study, we explore the potential of ensemble learning to enhance the performance of LLMs in source code vulnerability detection. We conduct comprehensive experiments involving five LLMs (i.e., DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B, CodeLlama-7B, CodeLlama-13B, CodeQwen1.5-7B, and StarCoder2-15B), using three ensemble strategies (i.e., Bagging, Boosting, and Stacking). These experiments are carried out across three widely adopted datasets (i.e., Devign, ReVeal, and BigVul). Inspired by Mixture of Experts (MoE) techniques, we further propose Dynamic Gated Stacking (DGS), a Stacking variant tailored for vulnerability detection. Our results demonstrate that ensemble approaches can significantly improve detection performance, with Boosting excelling in scenarios involving imbalanced datasets. Moreover, DGS consistently outperforms traditional Stacking, particularly in handling class imbalance and multi-class classification tasks. These findings offer valuable insights into building more reliable and effective LLM-based vulnerability detection systems through ensemble learning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12629","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:44:51Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:44:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-19T02:10:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12476","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12476","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12476","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sumanta","familyName":"Bhattacharyya","name":"Bhattacharyya, Sumanta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sara","familyName":"Riazi","name":"Riazi, Sara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedram","familyName":"Rooshenas","name":"Rooshenas, Pedram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Audited Reasoning Refinement: Fine-Tuning Language Models via LLM-Guided Step-Wise Evaluation and Correction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T21:47:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:10:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T22:28:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-19T00:09:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Training a task-specific small reasoning model is challenging when direct human supervision or high-quality labels are scarce. However, LLMs with reasoning capabilities produce abundant intermediate reasoning traces that can be systematically refined to create effective supervision signals. We propose Reason-Refine-then-Align (R2tA), which turns refined model rationales into supervision for training task-specific reasoning models. Our method generates initial reasoning and responses from an open-source base model on task-specific inputs, then refines these traces, fixing hallucinations and inconsistencies, to form a high-fidelity dataset. We perform a two-stage alignment, supervised fine-tuning (SFT), followed by direct preference optimization (DPO) to calibrate the model's intermediate reasoning with human-validated conceptual preferences and then condition the final output on that aligned reasoning. As a case study, we apply R2tA to evaluate extended entity relationship diagrams (EERDs) in database system design, a structurally complex task where prompt-only methods miss or hallucinate errors. We curated a dataset of 600 EERD variants (train/test split of 450/150, respectively) with induced mistakes spanning 11 categories. Empirical evaluation suggests R2tA provides a practical, cost-effective path to scalable LLM adaptation in data-scarce domains, enabling reproducible AI tools for education and beyond."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12476","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:41:17Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:41:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-19T02:09:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12912","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12912","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12912","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raphael","familyName":"Wenzel","name":"Wenzel, Raphael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Malte","familyName":"Probst","name":"Probst, Malte","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spotting the Unfriendly Robot -- Towards better Metrics for Interactions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T10:05:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T14:25:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Establishing standardized metrics for Social Robot Navigation (SRN) algorithms for assessing the quality and social compliance of robot behavior around humans is essential for SRN research. Currently, commonly used evaluation metrics lack the ability to quantify how cooperative an agent behaves in interaction with humans. Concretely, in a simple frontal approach scenario, no metric specifically captures if both agents cooperate or if one agent stays on collision course and the other agent is forced to evade. To address this limitation, we propose two new metrics, a conflict intensity metric and the responsibility metric. Together, these metrics are capable of evaluating the quality of human-robot interactions by showing how much a given algorithm has contributed to reducing a conflict and which agent actually took responsibility of the resolution. This work aims to contribute to the development of a comprehensive and standardized evaluation methodology for SRN, ultimately enhancing the safety, efficiency, and social acceptance of robots in human-centric environments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Presented at 2025 IEEE Conference on Robotics and Automation (ICRA) Workshop: Advances in Social Navigation: Planning, HRI and Beyond"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12912","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:51:29Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:51:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-19T02:08:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12577","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12577","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12577","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elinor","familyName":"Poole-Dayan","name":"Poole-Dayan, Elinor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deb","familyName":"Roy","name":"Roy, Deb","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jad","familyName":"Kabbara","name":"Kabbara, Jad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An AI-Powered Framework for Analyzing Collective Idea Evolution in Deliberative Assemblies"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T02:08:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:48:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In an era of increasing societal fragmentation, political polarization, and erosion of public trust in institutions, representative deliberative assemblies are emerging as a promising democratic forum for developing effective policy outcomes on complex global issues. Despite theoretical attention, there remains limited empirical work that systematically traces how specific ideas evolve, are prioritized, or are discarded during deliberation to form policy recommendations. Addressing these gaps, this work poses two central questions: (1) How might we trace the evolution and distillation of ideas into concrete recommendations within deliberative assemblies? (2) How does the deliberative process shape delegate perspectives and influence voting dynamics over the course of the assembly? To address these questions, we develop LLM-based methodologies for empirically analyzing transcripts from a tech-enhanced in-person deliberative assembly. The framework identifies and visualizes the space of expressed suggestions. We also empirically reconstruct each delegate's evolving perspective throughout the assembly. Our methods contribute novel empirical insights into deliberative processes and demonstrate how LLMs can surface high-resolution dynamics otherwise invisible in traditional assembly outputs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12577","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:43:38Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:43:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-19T02:08:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12229","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12229","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12229","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"MSR","familyName":"Avinash","name":"Avinash, MSR","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Profiling LoRA/QLoRA Fine-Tuning Efficiency on Consumer GPUs: An RTX 4060 Case Study"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-07T21:41:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T13:21:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fine-tuning large language models (LLMs) with parameter-efficient techniques such as LoRA and QLoRA has enabled adaptation of foundation models on modest hardware. Yet the efficiency of such training on consumer-grade GPUs, especially under strict 8 GB VRAM limits, remains underexplored. We present a controlled profiling study of LoRA/QLoRA fine-tuning using the Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct model on a single NVIDIA RTX 4060. Across three representative configurations, we systematically vary batch size, sequence length, optimizer choice (AdamW vs. PagedAdamW), and precision (fp16 vs. bf16). We report throughput (tokens/s), time per 10k tokens, and VRAM footprint, alongside energy estimates derived from GPU board power limits. Our results show that paged optimizers improve throughput by up to 25% (628 tok/s vs. 500 tok/s baseline), while bf16 degrades efficiency relative to fp16. Despite 8 GB constraints, sequence lengths up to 2048 tokens were feasible using parameter-efficient strategies. To our knowledge, this is the first systematic case study of LLM fine-tuning efficiency on consumer GPUs, providing reproducible benchmarks and practical guidelines for resource-constrained researchers and practitioners."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 3 figures, 2 tables. Primary category: cs.LG (Machine Learning); secondary: cs.AI (Artificial Intelligence). LaTeX source with figures included"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12229","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:35:40Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:35:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-19T02:08:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12824","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12824","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12824","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zechao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zechao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangkun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiangkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dapeng","familyName":"Lang","name":"Lang, Dapeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DiffHash: Text-Guided Targeted Attack via Diffusion Models against Deep Hashing Image Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T08:49:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:33:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T07:49:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:29:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep hashing models have been widely adopted to tackle the challenges of large-scale image retrieval. However, these approaches face serious security risks due to their vulnerability to adversarial examples. Despite the increasing exploration of targeted attacks on deep hashing models, existing approaches still suffer from a lack of multimodal guidance, reliance on labeling information and dependence on pixel-level operations for attacks. To address these limitations, we proposed DiffHash, a novel diffusion-based targeted attack for deep hashing. Unlike traditional pixel-based attacks that directly modify specific pixels and lack multimodal guidance, our approach focuses on optimizing the latent representations of images, guided by text information generated by a Large Language Model (LLM) for the target image. Furthermore, we designed a multi-space hash alignment network to align the high-dimension image space and text space to the low-dimension binary hash space. During reconstruction, we also incorporated text-guided attention mechanisms to refine adversarial examples, ensuring them aligned with the target semantics while maintaining visual plausibility. Extensive experiments have demonstrated that our method outperforms state-of-the-art (SOTA) targeted attack methods, achieving better black-box transferability and offering more excellent stability across datasets."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12824","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:49:25Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:49:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:46:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12747","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12747","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12747","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Botao","familyName":"He","name":"He, Botao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amir Hossein","familyName":"Shahidzadeh","name":"Shahidzadeh, Amir Hossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianrui","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Tianrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guofei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guofei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Howie","familyName":"Choset","name":"Choset, Howie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dinesh","familyName":"Manocha","name":"Manocha, Dinesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Glen","familyName":"Chou","name":"Chou, Glen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cornelia","familyName":"Fermuller","name":"Fermuller, Cornelia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiannis","familyName":"Aloimonos","name":"Aloimonos, Yiannis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NavMoE: Hybrid Model- and Learning-based Traversability Estimation for Local Navigation via Mixture of Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T07:07:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:28:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T06:31:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T03:00:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper explores traversability estimation for robot navigation. A key bottleneck in traversability estimation lies in efficiently achieving reliable and robust predictions while accurately encoding both geometric and semantic information across diverse environments. We introduce Navigation via Mixture of Experts (NAVMOE), a hierarchical and modular approach for traversability estimation and local navigation. NAVMOE combines multiple specialized models for specific terrain types, each of which can be either a classical model-based or a learning-based approach that predicts traversability for specific terrain types. NAVMOE dynamically weights the contributions of different models based on the input environment through a gating network. Overall, our approach offers three advantages: First, NAVMOE enables traversability estimation to adaptively leverage specialized approaches for different terrains, which enhances generalization across diverse and unseen environments. Second, our approach significantly improves efficiency with negligible cost of solution quality by introducing a training-free lazy gating mechanism, which is designed to minimize the number of activated experts during inference. Third, our approach uses a two-stage training strategy that enables the training for the gating networks within the hybrid MoE method that contains nondifferentiable modules. Extensive experiments show that NAVMOE delivers a better efficiency and performance balance than any individual expert or full ensemble across different domains, improving cross-domain generalization and reducing average computational cost by 81.2% via lazy gating, with less than a 2% loss in path quality."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12747","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:47:33Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:47:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:45:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12248","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12248","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12248","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuriel","familyName":"Ryan","name":"Ryan, Yuriel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui Yang","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Rui Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenny Tsu Wei","familyName":"Choo","name":"Choo, Kenny Tsu Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roy Ka-Wei","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Roy Ka-Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Humor in Pixels: Benchmarking Large Multimodal Models Understanding of Online Comics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T01:39:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:01:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T05:30:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:22:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding humor is a core aspect of social intelligence, yet it remains a significant challenge for Large Multimodal Models (LMMs). We introduce PixelHumor, a benchmark dataset of 2,800 annotated multi-panel comics designed to evaluate LMMs' ability to interpret multimodal humor and recognize narrative sequences. Experiments with state-of-the-art LMMs reveal substantial gaps: for instance, top models achieve only 61% accuracy in panel sequencing, far below human performance. This underscores critical limitations in current models' integration of visual and textual cues for coherent narrative and humor understanding. By providing a rigorous framework for evaluating multimodal contextual and narrative reasoning, PixelHumor aims to drive the development of LMMs that better engage in natural, socially aware interactions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 8 figures, EMNLP 2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12248","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:36:06Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:36:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:44:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13164","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13164","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13164","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haowei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Haowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xintao","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Xintao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henry X.","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Henry X.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TeraSim-World: Worldwide Safety-Critical Data Synthesis for End-to-End Autonomous Driving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T15:14:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:53:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T04:40:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:21:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Safe and scalable deployment of end-to-end (E2E) autonomous driving requires extensive and diverse data, particularly safety-critical events. Existing data are mostly generated from simulators with a significant sim-to-real gap or collected from on-road testing that is costly and unsafe. This paper presents TeraSim-World, an automated pipeline that synthesizes realistic and geographically diverse safety-critical data for E2E autonomous driving at anywhere in the world. Starting from an arbitrary location, TeraSim-World retrieves real-world maps and traffic demand from geospatial data sources. Then, it simulates agent behaviors from naturalistic driving datasets, and orchestrates diverse adversities to create corner cases. Informed by street views of the same location, it achieves photorealistic, geographically grounded sensor rendering via the frontier video generation model Cosmos-Drive. By bridging agent and sensor simulations, TeraSim-World provides a scalable and critical data synthesis framework for training and evaluation of E2E autonomous driving systems. Codes and videos are available at https://wjiawei.com/terasim-world-web/ ."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13164","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:57:24Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:57:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:44:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13317","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13317","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13317","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"An-Chieh","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, An-Chieh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yukang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhijian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaolong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Subhashree","familyName":"Radhakrishnan","name":"Radhakrishnan, Subhashree","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Song","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Song","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Kautz","name":"Kautz, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pavlo","familyName":"Molchanov","name":"Molchanov, Pavlo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongxu","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Hongxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaolong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sifei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Sifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"3D Aware Region Prompted Vision Language Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T17:59:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T01:00:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Spatial Region 3D (SR-3D) aware vision-language model that connects single-view 2D images and multi-view 3D data through a shared visual token space. SR-3D supports flexible region prompting, allowing users to annotate regions with bounding boxes, segmentation masks on any frame, or directly in 3D, without the need for exhaustive multi-frame labeling. We achieve this by enriching 2D visual features with 3D positional embeddings, which allows the 3D model to draw upon strong 2D priors for more accurate spatial reasoning across frames, even when objects of interest do not co-occur within the same view. Extensive experiments on both general 2D vision language and specialized 3D spatial benchmarks demonstrate that SR-3D achieves state-of-the-art performance, underscoring its effectiveness for unifying 2D and 3D representation space on scene understanding. Moreover, we observe applicability to in-the-wild videos without sensory 3D inputs or ground-truth 3D annotations, where SR-3D accurately infers spatial relationships and metric measurements."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project Website: https://www.anjiecheng.me/sr3d"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13317","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T02:01:01Z","registered":"2025-09-17T02:01:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T02:01:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13311","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13311","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13311","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runnan","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Runnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shihao","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Shihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baixuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Baixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jialong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Guangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbiao","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Wenbiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaobin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaobin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangcai","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Liangcai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shibin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Shibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengwei","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Zhengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengjun","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Pengjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingren","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jingren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards General Agentic Intelligence via Environment Scaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T17:57:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:59:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Advanced agentic intelligence is a prerequisite for deploying Large Language Models in practical, real-world applications. Diverse real-world APIs demand precise, robust function-calling intelligence, which needs agents to develop these capabilities through interaction in varied environments. The breadth of function-calling competence is closely tied to the diversity of environments in which agents are trained. In this work, we scale up environments as a step towards advancing general agentic intelligence. This gives rise to two central challenges: (i) how to scale environments in a principled manner, and (ii) how to effectively train agentic capabilities from experiences derived through interactions with these environments. To address these, we design a scalable framework that automatically constructs heterogeneous environments that are fully simulated, systematically broadening the space of function-calling scenarios. We further adapt a two-phase agent fine-tuning strategy: first endowing agents with fundamental agentic capabilities, then specializing them for domain-specific contexts. Extensive experiments on agentic benchmarks, tau-bench, tau2-Bench, and ACEBench, demonstrate that our trained model, AgentScaler, significantly enhances the function-calling capability of models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"https://tongyi-agent.github.io/blog/introducing-tongyi-deep-research/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13311","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T02:00:52Z","registered":"2025-09-17T02:00:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T02:00:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13310","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13310","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13310","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangcai","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Liangcai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Guangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chenxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maojia","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Maojia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jialong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanzhong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xuanzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zile","familyName":"Qiao","name":"Qiao, Zile","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongwang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhongwang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huifeng","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Huifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shihao","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Shihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runnan","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Runnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengwei","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Zhengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbiao","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Wenbiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxiong","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Chenxiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengjun","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Pengjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingren","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jingren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scaling Agents via Continual Pre-training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T17:57:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:59:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have evolved into agentic systems capable of autonomous tool use and multi-step reasoning for complex problem-solving. However, post-training approaches building upon general-purpose foundation models consistently underperform in agentic tasks, particularly in open-source implementations. We identify the root cause: the absence of robust agentic foundation models forces models during post-training to simultaneously learn diverse agentic behaviors while aligning them to expert demonstrations, thereby creating fundamental optimization tensions. To this end, we are the first to propose incorporating Agentic Continual Pre-training (Agentic CPT) into the deep research agents training pipeline to build powerful agentic foundational models. Based on this approach, we develop a deep research agent model named AgentFounder. We evaluate our AgentFounder-30B on 10 benchmarks and achieve state-of-the-art performance while retains strong tool-use ability, notably 39.9% on BrowseComp-en, 43.3% on BrowseComp-zh, and 31.5% Pass@1 on HLE."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"https://tongyi-agent.github.io/blog/introducing-tongyi-deep-research/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13310","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T02:00:51Z","registered":"2025-09-17T02:00:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T02:00:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13305","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13305","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13305","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongwang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhongwang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huifeng","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Huifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yida","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yida","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liwen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Liwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Litu","familyName":"Ou","name":"Ou, Litu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dingchu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Dingchu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xixi","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xixi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jialong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zile","familyName":"Qiao","name":"Qiao, Zile","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengjun","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Pengjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingren","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jingren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WebSailor-V2: Bridging the Chasm to Proprietary Agents via Synthetic Data and Scalable Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T17:57:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:59:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transcending human cognitive limitations represents a critical frontier in LLM training. Proprietary agentic systems like DeepResearch have demonstrated superhuman capabilities on extremely complex information-seeking benchmarks such as BrowseComp, a feat previously unattainable. We posit that their success hinges on a sophisticated reasoning pattern absent in open-source models: the ability to systematically reduce extreme uncertainty when navigating vast information landscapes. Based on this insight, we introduce WebSailor, a complete post-training methodology designed to instill this crucial capability. Our approach involves generating novel, high-uncertainty tasks through structured sampling and information obfuscation, RFT cold start, and an efficient agentic RL training algorithm, Duplicating Sampling Policy Optimization (DUPO). With this integrated pipeline, WebSailor significantly outperforms all open-source agents in complex information-seeking tasks, matching proprietary agents' performance and closing the capability gap."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"https://tongyi-agent.github.io/blog/introducing-tongyi-deep-research/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13305","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T02:00:44Z","registered":"2025-09-17T02:00:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T02:00:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13289","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13289","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13289","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lovish","familyName":"Kaushik","name":"Kaushik, Lovish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Agnij","familyName":"Biswas","name":"Biswas, Agnij","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Somdyuti","familyName":"Paul","name":"Paul, Somdyuti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Image Realness Assessment and Localization with Multimodal Features"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T17:42:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:59:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A reliable method of quantifying the perceptual realness of AI-generated images and identifying visually inconsistent regions is crucial for practical use of AI-generated images and for improving photorealism of generative AI via realness feedback during training. This paper introduces a framework that accomplishes both overall objective realness assessment and local inconsistency identification of AI-generated images using textual descriptions of visual inconsistencies generated by vision-language models trained on large datasets that serve as reliable substitutes for human annotations. Our results demonstrate that the proposed multimodal approach improves objective realness prediction performance and produces dense realness maps that effectively distinguish between realistic and unrealistic spatial regions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13289","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T02:00:19Z","registered":"2025-09-17T02:00:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T02:00:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13288","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13288","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13288","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marjorie","familyName":"McShane","name":"McShane, Marjorie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergei","familyName":"Nirenburg","name":"Nirenburg, Sergei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanjay","familyName":"Oruganti","name":"Oruganti, Sanjay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesse","familyName":"English","name":"English, Jesse","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Shapes of Cognition for Computational Cognitive Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T17:39:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:58:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Shapes of cognition is a new conceptual paradigm for the computational cognitive modeling of Language-Endowed Intelligent Agents (LEIAs). Shapes are remembered constellations of sensory, linguistic, conceptual, episodic, and procedural knowledge that allow agents to cut through the complexity of real life the same way as people do: by expecting things to be typical, recognizing patterns, acting by habit, reasoning by analogy, satisficing, and generally minimizing cognitive load to the degree situations permit. Atypical outcomes are treated using shapes-based recovery methods, such as learning on the fly, asking a human partner for help, or seeking an actionable, even if imperfect, situational understanding. Although shapes is an umbrella term, it is not vague: shapes-based modeling involves particular objectives, hypotheses, modeling strategies, knowledge bases, and actual models of wide-ranging phenomena, all implemented within a particular cognitive architecture. Such specificity is needed both to vet our hypotheses and to achieve our practical aims of building useful agent systems that are explainable, extensible, and worthy of our trust, even in critical domains. However, although the LEIA example of shapes-based modeling is specific, the principles can be applied more broadly, giving new life to knowledge-based and hybrid AI."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13288","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T02:00:18Z","registered":"2025-09-17T02:00:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T02:00:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13282","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13282","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13282","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Salamatian","name":"Salamatian, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amirhossein","familyName":"Abaskohi","name":"Abaskohi, Amirhossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wan-Cyuan","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Wan-Cyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mir Rayat Imtiaz","familyName":"Hossain","name":"Hossain, Mir Rayat Imtiaz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leonid","familyName":"Sigal","name":"Sigal, Leonid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giuseppe","familyName":"Carenini","name":"Carenini, Giuseppe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ChartGaze: Enhancing Chart Understanding in LVLMs with Eye-Tracking Guided Attention Refinement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T17:35:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:58:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Charts are a crucial visual medium for communicating and representing information. While Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) have made progress on chart question answering (CQA), the task remains challenging, particularly when models attend to irrelevant regions of the chart. In this work, we present ChartGaze, a new eye-tracking dataset that captures human gaze patterns during chart reasoning tasks. Through a systematic comparison of human and model attention, we find that LVLMs often diverge from human gaze, leading to reduced interpretability and accuracy. To address this, we propose a gaze-guided attention refinement that aligns image-text attention with human fixations. Our approach improves both answer accuracy and attention alignment, yielding gains of up to 2.56 percentage points across multiple models. These results demonstrate the promise of incorporating human gaze to enhance both the reasoning quality and interpretability of chart-focused LVLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13282","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T02:00:09Z","registered":"2025-09-17T02:00:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T02:00:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13268","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13268","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13268","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rodrigo M","familyName":"Carrillo-Larco","name":"Carrillo-Larco, Rodrigo M","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLMs for energy and macronutrients estimation using only text data from 24-hour dietary recalls: a parameter-efficient fine-tuning experiment using a 10-shot prompt"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T17:26:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:58:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"BACKGROUND: Most artificial intelligence tools used to estimate nutritional content rely on image input. However, whether large language models (LLMs) can accurately predict nutritional values based solely on text descriptions of foods consumed remains unknown. If effective, this approach could enable simpler dietary monitoring without the need for photographs. METHODS: We used 24-hour dietary recalls from adolescents aged 12-19 years in the National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES). An open-source quantized LLM was prompted using a 10-shot, chain-of-thought approach to estimate energy and five macronutrients based solely on text strings listing foods and their quantities. We then applied parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) to evaluate whether predictive accuracy improved. NHANES-calculated values served as the ground truth for energy, proteins, carbohydrates, total sugar, dietary fiber and total fat. RESULTS: In a pooled dataset of 11,281 adolescents (49.9% male, mean age 15.4 years), the vanilla LLM yielded poor predictions. The mean absolute error (MAE) was 652.08 for energy and the Lin's CCC \u0026lt;0.46 across endpoints. In contrast, the fine-tuned model performed substantially better, with energy MAEs ranging from 171.34 to 190.90 across subsets, and Lin's CCC exceeding 0.89 for all outcomes. CONCLUSIONS: When prompted using a chain-of-thought approach and fine-tuned with PEFT, open-source LLMs exposed solely to text input can accurately predict energy and macronutrient values from 24-hour dietary recalls. This approach holds promise for low-burden, text-based dietary monitoring tools."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"https://github.com/rodrigo-carrillo/LLMs-Macronutrient-Estimation-NHANES-Adolescents"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13268","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:59:49Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:59:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:59:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13266","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13266","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13266","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaobing","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Xiaobing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaokun","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Zhaokun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenqiang","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Wenqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenghao","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Zhenghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"JANUS: A Dual-Constraint Generative Framework for Stealthy Node Injection Attacks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T17:24:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:58:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) have demonstrated remarkable performance across various applications, yet they are vulnerable to sophisticated adversarial attacks, particularly node injection attacks. The success of such attacks heavily relies on their stealthiness, the ability to blend in with the original graph and evade detection. However, existing methods often achieve stealthiness by relying on indirect proxy metrics, lacking consideration for the fundamental characteristics of the injected content, or focusing only on imitating local structures, which leads to the problem of local myopia. To overcome these limitations, we propose a dual-constraint stealthy node injection framework, called Joint Alignment of Nodal and Universal Structures (JANUS). At the local level, we introduce a local feature manifold alignment strategy to achieve geometric consistency in the feature space. At the global level, we incorporate structured latent variables and maximize the mutual information with the generated structures, ensuring the injected structures are consistent with the semantic patterns of the original graph. We model the injection attack as a sequential decision process, which is optimized by a reinforcement learning agent. Experiments on multiple standard datasets demonstrate that the JANUS framework significantly outperforms existing methods in terms of both attack effectiveness and stealthiness."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13266","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:59:46Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:59:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:59:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13265","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13265","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13265","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yukun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"TianYang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, TianYang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Private or Public: Large Language Models as Quasi-Public Goods in the AI Economy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T17:22:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:57:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper conceptualizes Large Language Models (LLMs) as a form of mixed public goods within digital infrastructure, analyzing their economic properties through a comprehensive theoretical framework. We develop mathematical models to quantify the non-rivalry characteristics, partial excludability, and positive externalities of LLMs. Through comparative analysis of open-source and closed-source development paths, we identify systematic differences in resource allocation efficiency, innovation trajectories, and access equity. Our empirical research evaluates the spillover effects and network externalities of LLMs across different domains, including knowledge diffusion, innovation acceleration, and industry transformation. Based on these findings, we propose policy recommendations for balancing innovation incentives with equitable access, including public-private partnership mechanisms, computational resource democratization, and governance structures that optimize social welfare. This interdisciplinary approach contributes to understanding the economic nature of foundation AI models and provides policy guidance for their development as critical digital infrastructure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13265","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:59:45Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:59:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:59:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13255","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13255","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13255","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mattia","familyName":"Soldan","name":"Soldan, Mattia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabian Caba","familyName":"Heilbron","name":"Heilbron, Fabian Caba","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bernard","familyName":"Ghanem","name":"Ghanem, Bernard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josef","familyName":"Sivic","name":"Sivic, Josef","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bryan","familyName":"Russell","name":"Russell, Bryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ResidualViT for Efficient Temporally Dense Video Encoding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T17:12:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:57:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Several video understanding tasks, such as natural language temporal video grounding, temporal activity localization, and audio description generation, require \"temporally dense\" reasoning over frames sampled at high temporal resolution. However, computing frame-level features for these tasks is computationally expensive given the temporal resolution requirements. In this paper, we make three contributions to reduce the cost of computing features for temporally dense tasks. First, we introduce a vision transformer (ViT) architecture, dubbed ResidualViT, that leverages the large temporal redundancy in videos to efficiently compute temporally dense frame-level features. Our architecture incorporates (i) learnable residual connections that ensure temporal consistency across consecutive frames and (ii) a token reduction module that enhances processing speed by selectively discarding temporally redundant information while reusing weights of a pretrained foundation model. Second, we propose a lightweight distillation strategy to approximate the frame-level features of the original foundation model. Finally, we evaluate our approach across four tasks and five datasets, in both zero-shot and fully supervised settings, demonstrating significant reductions in computational cost (up to 60%) and improvements in inference speed (up to 2.5x faster), all while closely approximating the accuracy of the original foundation model."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13255","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:59:30Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:59:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:59:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13244","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13244","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13244","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianfeng","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Jianfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julina","familyName":"Maharjan","name":"Maharjan, Julina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karin G.","familyName":"Coifman","name":"Coifman, Karin G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoming","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Ruoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating LLM Alignment on Personality Inference from Real-World Interview Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T16:54:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:56:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in roles requiring nuanced psychological understanding, such as emotional support agents, counselors, and decision-making assistants. However, their ability to interpret human personality traits, a critical aspect of such applications, remains unexplored, particularly in ecologically valid conversational settings. While prior work has simulated LLM \"personas\" using discrete Big Five labels on social media data, the alignment of LLMs with continuous, ground-truth personality assessments derived from natural interactions is largely unexamined. To address this gap, we introduce a novel benchmark comprising semi-structured interview transcripts paired with validated continuous Big Five trait scores. Using this dataset, we systematically evaluate LLM performance across three paradigms: (1) zero-shot and chain-of-thought prompting with GPT-4.1 Mini, (2) LoRA-based fine-tuning applied to both RoBERTa and Meta-LLaMA architectures, and (3) regression using static embeddings from pretrained BERT and OpenAI's text-embedding-3-small. Our results reveal that all Pearson correlations between model predictions and ground-truth personality traits remain below 0.26, highlighting the limited alignment of current LLMs with validated psychological constructs. Chain-of-thought prompting offers minimal gains over zero-shot, suggesting that personality inference relies more on latent semantic representation than explicit reasoning. These findings underscore the challenges of aligning LLMs with complex human attributes and motivate future work on trait-specific prompting, context-aware modeling, and alignment-oriented fine-tuning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13244","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:59:15Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:59:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:59:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13239","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13239","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13239","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianxu","familyName":"An","name":"An, Tianxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Flavio","familyName":"De Vincenti","name":"De Vincenti, Flavio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuntao","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yuntao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Hutter","name":"Hutter, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stelian","familyName":"Coros","name":"Coros, Stelian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Collaborative Loco-Manipulation for Pick-and-Place Tasks with Dynamic Reward Curriculum"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T16:45:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:56:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a hierarchical RL pipeline for training one-armed legged robots to perform pick-and-place (P\u0026amp;P) tasks end-to-end -- from approaching the payload to releasing it at a target area -- in both single-robot and cooperative dual-robot settings. We introduce a novel dynamic reward curriculum that enables a single policy to efficiently learn long-horizon P\u0026amp;P operations by progressively guiding the agents through payload-centered sub-objectives. Compared to state-of-the-art approaches for long-horizon RL tasks, our method improves training efficiency by 55% and reduces execution time by 18.6% in simulation experiments. In the dual-robot case, we show that our policy enables each robot to attend to different components of its observation space at distinct task stages, promoting effective coordination via autonomous attention shifts. We validate our method through real-world experiments using ANYmal D platforms in both single- and dual-robot scenarios. To our knowledge, this is the first RL pipeline that tackles the full scope of collaborative P\u0026amp;P with two legged manipulators."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13239","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:59:08Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:59:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:59:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13237","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13237","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13237","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aniket","familyName":"Didolkar","name":"Didolkar, Aniket","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolas","familyName":"Ballas","name":"Ballas, Nicolas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanjeev","familyName":"Arora","name":"Arora, Sanjeev","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anirudh","familyName":"Goyal","name":"Goyal, Anirudh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Metacognitive Reuse: Turning Recurring LLM Reasoning Into Concise Behaviors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T16:44:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:56:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) now solve multi-step problems by emitting extended chains of thought. During the process, they often re-derive the same intermediate steps across problems, inflating token usage and latency. This saturation of the context window leaves less capacity for exploration. We study a simple mechanism that converts recurring reasoning fragments into concise, reusable \"behaviors\" (name + instruction) via the model's own metacognitive analysis of prior traces. These behaviors are stored in a \"behavior handbook\" which supplies them to the model in-context at inference or distills them into parameters via supervised fine-tuning. This approach achieves improved test-time reasoning across three different settings - 1) Behavior-conditioned inference: Providing the LLM relevant behaviors in-context during reasoning reduces number of reasoning tokens by up to 46% while matching or improving baseline accuracy; 2) Behavior-guided self-improvement: Without any parameter updates, the model improves its own future reasoning by leveraging behaviors from its own past problem solving attempts. This yields up to 10% higher accuracy than a naive critique-and-revise baseline; and 3) Behavior-conditioned SFT: SFT on behavior-conditioned reasoning traces is more effective at converting non-reasoning models into reasoning models as compared to vanilla SFT. Together, these results indicate that turning slow derivations into fast procedural hints enables LLMs to remember how to reason, not just what to conclude."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 9 Figures, 5 Tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13237","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:59:05Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:59:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:59:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13235","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13235","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13235","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linyue","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Linyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyang","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Yuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoding","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Xiaoding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huiming","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Scenario-Driven Cognitive Approach to Next-Generation AI Memory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T16:43:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:56:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As artificial intelligence advances toward artificial general intelligence (AGI), the need for robust and human-like memory systems has become increasingly evident. Current memory architectures often suffer from limited adaptability, insufficient multimodal integration, and an inability to support continuous learning. To address these limitations, we propose a scenario-driven methodology that extracts essential functional requirements from representative cognitive scenarios, leading to a unified set of design principles for next-generation AI memory systems. Based on this approach, we introduce the \\textbf{COgnitive Layered Memory Architecture (COLMA)}, a novel framework that integrates cognitive scenarios, memory processes, and storage mechanisms into a cohesive design. COLMA provides a structured foundation for developing AI systems capable of lifelong learning and human-like reasoning, thereby contributing to the pragmatic development of AGI."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13235","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:59:02Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:59:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:59:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13234","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13234","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13234","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nadim","familyName":"Barakat","name":"Barakat, Nadim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William","familyName":"Lotter","name":"Lotter, William","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Simulating Clinical AI Assistance using Multimodal LLMs: A Case Study in Diabetic Retinopathy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T16:42:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:56:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diabetic retinopathy (DR) is a leading cause of blindness worldwide, and AI systems can expand access to fundus photography screening. Current FDA-cleared systems primarily provide binary referral outputs, where this minimal output may limit clinical trust and utility. Yet, determining the most effective output format to enhance clinician-AI performance is an empirical challenge that is difficult to assess at scale. We evaluated multimodal large language models (MLLMs) for DR detection and their ability to simulate clinical AI assistance across different output types. Two models were tested on IDRiD and Messidor-2: GPT-4o, a general-purpose MLLM, and MedGemma, an open-source medical model. Experiments included: (1) baseline evaluation, (2) simulated AI assistance with synthetic predictions, and (3) actual AI-to-AI collaboration where GPT-4o incorporated MedGemma outputs. MedGemma outperformed GPT-4o at baseline, achieving higher sensitivity and AUROC, while GPT-4o showed near-perfect specificity but low sensitivity. Both models adjusted predictions based on simulated AI inputs, but GPT-4o's performance collapsed with incorrect ones, whereas MedGemma remained more stable. In actual collaboration, GPT-4o achieved strong results when guided by MedGemma's descriptive outputs, even without direct image access (AUROC up to 0.96). These findings suggest MLLMs may improve DR screening pipelines and serve as scalable simulators for studying clinical AI assistance across varying output configurations. Open, lightweight models such as MedGemma may be especially valuable in low-resource settings, while descriptive outputs could enhance explainability and clinician trust in clinical workflows."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13234","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:59:01Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:59:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:59:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":281,"totalPages":12,"page":5},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=5\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=25\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-16T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-17T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+agent+OR+agents+OR+diffusion+OR+multimodal+OR+GPU+OR+MoE+OR+%22state+space%22+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22vision+language%22%29","next":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=6\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=25\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-16T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-17T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+agent+OR+agents+OR+diffusion+OR+multimodal+OR+GPU+OR+MoE+OR+%22state+space%22+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22vision+language%22%29"}}